% Abstract
\begin{abstract}
This dissertation develops a post-quantum cryptographic verification framework 
for multi-domain physical simulations. The work integrates ML-KEM-1024 
encapsulation, CKKS homomorphic tensor evolution, and zero-knowledge proofs 
into a unified verification pipeline. The resulting system ensures that 
general relativity, quantum mechanics, electromagnetism, and nuclear physics 
can be evolved under encryption while maintaining conservation-law integrity 
and cross-domain consistency. This establishes a foundation for secure 
cosmological computation in the post-quantum era.
\end{abstract}

\chapter{Introduction}
Modern physics engines rely on plaintext numerical evolution, exposing 
sensitive scientific data to potential quantum adversaries. This dissertation 
introduces a post-quantum cryptographic verification framework capable of 
evolving physical systems under encryption while preserving mathematical 
fidelity. The approach combines lattice-based cryptography, homomorphic 
tensor evolution, and zero-knowledge verification.

